  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 134 PM MDT Sat Sep 26 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nAlthough lingering cyclonic flow aloft will be in place across the \nregion through the remainder of the day, cooler surface temperatures \nwill keep showery precipitation at bay. Troughing presently off \nthe west coast of the CONUS will continue diving SE toward CA by \nSunday. A southwesterly flow aloft ahead of this trough will yield\nample cloud cover through the day Sunday, with a low-end \nopportunity for a few light showers, particularly Sunday evening. \nLingering influence from troughing will yield another opportunity \nfor showers and perhaps a few thunderstorms in the vicinity of \nWest Yellowstone Monday.\n\nA zonal flow aloft develops after Monday as the aforementioned \ntroughing continues southeastward toward the four-corners region. \nTemperatures trend slightly warmer as a result, with breezy \nstretch developing over the plains Tuesday and Wednesday. Most, if\nnot all of the upcoming week is looking dry. -AM\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nCross barrier flow looks to peak sometime on Tuesday. The \nprobability for a 55 mph gust around Cut Bank is in the vicinity of \n25%. \n\nA few deterministic runs continue to hint at a trough moving across \nthe region late week into the weekend, but ensemble support is \nminimal. Hence, A dry scenario is favored next weekend, with around \na 20% chance for a trend toward a wetter scenario. -AM",
